'use strict';
const {course}=require('./data-helper');
const specs=[
{id:'ps-conditional',comps:['m1.c1'],title:'Define a probability space and condition on evidence',intro:'A university wants to understand the chance that an application is complete after a reminder. Start by defining the population and event rather than dividing whichever counts happen to be available.',sections:[['Events and denominators','An event is a set of possible outcomes. P(A) describes its probability under a stated sampling process. Conditional probability P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B), provided P(B)>0, restricts attention to outcomes where B occurs. It is not generally equal to P(B|A).','A table can estimate conditional proportions when its rows and selection rules match the target question. Count the intersection in the numerator and the conditioning event in the denominator. With zero conditioning observations, the empirical fraction is undefined; return that limitation instead of a fabricated zero.'],['Independence is an assumption to evaluate','A and B are independent when P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B). Conditional on B, the chance of A then stays the same when P(B)>0. Independence is not the same as mutually exclusive: two positive-probability mutually exclusive events cannot both occur and are dependent.','The fixture uses exact rational counts to make denominators inspectable. It describes associations in this fixed synthetic population; it does not prove a reminder caused completion or represent applicants missing from the records.']],problem:'Compute completion among reminded applicants and distinguish the reverse condition.',steps:['Count the intersection of reminder and completion.','Choose each conditioning population explicitly.','Calculate both exact fractions.'],code:'from fractions import Fraction\nreminded=40\ncomplete=50\nboth=30\nprint(Fraction(both,reminded))\nprint(Fraction(both,complete))',expect:'3/4\n3/5',limit:'The two fractions answer different descriptive questions. They do not establish causality or correct selection bias.',mistakes:['Reversing the condition changes the denominator and question.','Zero observed conditioning cases do not imply a true conditional probability of zero.'],takeaways:['State events and the sampling population.','Conditional probability restricts the reference population.'],practice:[
['Which denominator belongs in P(A|B)?','P(B)','P(A)','P(A∩B) always','P(A)+P(B)','Conditioning restricts the population to event B, so its probability is the divisor.'],
['What is required for the usual conditional-probability formula?','P(B)>0','P(A)=1','A and B are disjoint','Every outcome is equally likely','Division by P(B) requires a nonzero conditioning probability.'],
['Are P(A|B) and P(B|A) generally equal?','No','Yes by notation','Only if A is rare','Only for positive counts','The denominators and target events differ; equality requires particular conditions.'],
['Thirty of forty reminded applicants complete. What is the observed conditional rate?','75%','60%','40%','30%','The conditioning population is forty reminded applicants, and 30/40=0.75.'],
['A group has no observed members in a dataset. How should its empirical completion rate be reported?','Undefined from these counts','Exactly zero percent','Exactly one hundred percent','Equal to the population rate','A zero denominator cannot support an empirical rate without additional assumptions or data.'],
['An analyst calls two disjoint positive-probability events independent. What is wrong?','Their intersection is zero','Their names are different','Their counts are integers','Their probabilities sum below one','Independence would require a positive product, while disjoint events have zero intersection.'],
['A reminder group has a higher completion rate. What extra evidence is needed to claim causation?','A causal study design','A larger displayed percentage','A rounded conditional rate','A renamed outcome field','Observed conditional association may reflect selection, confounding or other differences rather than the reminder effect.']
],formal:[
['Which expression gives P(A|B) when P(B)>0?','P(A∩B)/P(B)','P(A)/P(B) always','P(B)/P(A∩B)','P(A)+P(B)','The intersection counts outcomes satisfying both events within the conditioning population B.'],
['What is the complement of an event with probability 0.2?','0.8','0.2','1.2','0','Complementary probabilities sum to one.'],
['What characterizes independence?','P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B)','A and B never coincide','P(A)=P(B) always','P(A|B)=1 always','Independent event probabilities multiply to give their joint probability.'],
['A support team resolves 18 of 24 escalated tickets. Which rate answers resolution given escalation?','18/24','18/all users','24/18','All tickets/24','The denominator must be the escalated population, not the entire customer base or a reversed ratio.'],
['A retailer counts paid orders among mobile sessions but divides by all sessions. What is mismatched?','The conditioning population','The existence of currency','The definition of addition','The ordering of identifiers','A mobile-conditional rate needs a denominator restricted to qualifying mobile sessions.'],
['A report estimates P(delayed|rural) and labels it P(rural|delayed). What changed?','The conditioning direction','Only numerical precision','Only the event spelling','Nothing mathematically','Reversing the condition exchanges the reference population and generally changes the value.'],
['A team has zero sampled canceled orders and reports a return rate among canceled orders. What is supported?','No empirical rate is available','The rate must equal zero','The rate must equal one','The rate must equal one half','With no conditioning observations the denominator is zero, so the sample cannot estimate that fraction directly.'],
['An incident is independent of a maintenance window under a stated model. What follows?','The chance is unchanged','The incident cannot occur','The window causes the incident','The probabilities must be equal','Independence means the event probability stays unchanged when conditioning on a positive-probability window.'],
['A survey excludes nonresponders but claims a rate for all customers. What needs review?','Target/selection fit','Only output decimal places','Only the chart title','Only row sorting','The recorded conditional population may differ from the intended population because response is selective.'],
['A dashboard combines two events as disjoint and independent, both with positive probability. Can both claims hold?','No, the product is nonzero','Yes for every survey','Yes if names differ','Only with integer counts','Disjointness gives zero joint probability, while independence gives a positive product in this case.']
]},
{id:'ps-baserates',comps:['m1.c2'],title:'Use Bayes’ rule without losing the base rate',intro:'An alert with high sensitivity can still be wrong often when the underlying event is rare. Base rates connect test behavior to the decisions a business actually faces.',sections:[['Reverse conditioning with all required terms','Bayes’ rule gives P(D|+)=[P(+|D)P(D)]/P(+). If D and its complement partition the population, P(+)=P(+|D)P(D)+P(+|not D)P(not D). Sensitivity is P(+|D); the false-positive rate is P(+|not D). Neither alone is precision P(D|+).','A natural-frequency table often makes this clearer than memorizing a formula. In 10,000 cases with prevalence 1%, 90% sensitivity finds 90 true positives. A 5% false-positive rate on 9,900 nonevents adds 495 false positives, so only 90/585 alerts correspond to the event.'],['Use the prevalence appropriate to the decision','A sample enriched with positive cases can estimate some conditional test properties but not directly the deployment prevalence or predictive value. Changes across populations and time can alter precision even if sensitivity and false-positive rate remain similar.','Threshold choices should consider investigation capacity, missed-event cost and false-alarm cost. A posterior estimate is not automatic permission for a high-impact action; uncertainty, calibration and review policy matter. The fixture models declared rates exactly, not a deployed medical or fraud system.']],problem:'Construct alert counts from a rare-event base rate and compute precision.',steps:['Separate event and nonevent populations.','Apply sensitivity and false-positive rate to their own denominators.','Divide true positives by every positive alert.'],code:'from fractions import Fraction\nevents,nonevents=100,9900\ntp=events*90//100\nfp=nonevents*5//100\nprint(tp,fp)\nprint(Fraction(tp,tp+fp))\nprint(round(tp/(tp+fp),4))',expect:'90 495\n2/13\n0.1538',limit:'The expected counts follow fixed assumed rates; they do not measure real-world calibration, fairness or uncertainty in estimated rates.',mistakes:['Sensitivity is not the probability an alert is a true event.','Ignoring prevalence can severely overstate predictive value.'],takeaways:['Bayes combines likelihood and prior prevalence.','Use rates from the deployment population when making operational decisions.'],practice:[
['What is sensitivity?','P(positive|event)','P(event|positive)','P(no event|negative)','P(event) alone','Sensitivity conditions on actual events and measures how often the test flags them.'],
['What is alert precision?','True positives/all positives','True positives/all events','False positives/all events','All alerts/all customers','Precision uses every positive alert in its denominator.'],
['What is the base rate in this setting?','Event prevalence','Only false alarms','Only sensitivity','The alert display rate','The base rate is the underlying fraction of cases with the event before using the test evidence.'],
['There are 90 true and 495 false positive alerts. What fraction of alerts is true?','90/585','90/100','495/9900','90/10000','Every positive alert contributes to the denominator, yielding about fifteen percent.'],
['A classifier is evaluated on a dataset with half fraud cases but deployed at 1% fraud. What cannot be copied directly?','The sample precision','The label definition alone','The feature count alone','The model version string','Predictive value depends on prevalence; an enriched evaluation sample changes that denominator.'],
['Prevalence falls while sensitivity and false-positive rate stay fixed. What often happens to precision?','It falls','It becomes sensitivity','It must become one','It stays fixed by definition','Fewer true events relative to nonevents reduces the positive-alert true-event proportion.'],
['A team plans high-impact action from an uncalibrated alert alone. What is missing?','A validated decision policy','Only a shorter alert label','Only higher screen contrast','Only a count of features','Operational decisions need calibrated evidence, error costs, capacity and appropriate review safeguards.']
],formal:[
['Which quantity directly incorporates prevalence when reversing a test condition?','Bayes posterior','Sensitivity alone','Specificity alone','Feature count','Bayes’ rule weights conditional test likelihoods by prior event prevalence.'],
['What is a false-positive rate?','P(positive|no event)','P(no event|positive)','P(event|negative)','P(positive) always','False-positive rate conditions on actual nonevents, not on positive alerts.'],
['Why use natural-frequency tables?','They expose denominators','They remove all uncertainty','They prove causality','They force equal prevalence','Expected event/nonevent counts make joint and conditional populations explicit.'],
['A rare disease screen is 99% sensitive. What is still needed to estimate positive predictive value?','Prevalence and false rate','Only the test name','Only the hospital size','Only the result font','Sensitivity alone cannot determine the mix of true and false positive results.'],
['A fraud review queue overflows despite high sensitivity. Which evidence helps?','Expected alert volume','Only the accuracy headline','Only model file size','Only a feature glossary','Queue load depends on true and false alerts across the actual population and operating threshold.'],
['A security model has few false positives per nonevent but billions of nonevents. What can occur?','Many absolute false alerts','No false alerts are possible','Precision must equal one','Every event is detected','A small per-case false-positive rate multiplied by a huge nonevent population can produce large alert volume.'],
['A team uses P(alert|fraud) as P(fraud|alert). What is omitted?','The base-rate calculation','Only numerical rounding','Only the sample identifier','The meaning of multiplication','The reverse conditional requires prevalence and the overall alert probability.'],
['A benchmark balances classes by design. What deployment property remains to estimate separately?','Real event prevalence','The existence of labels','The number of columns','The fact that rows are sampled','Balanced selection does not represent the real-world event mixture unless deployment is similarly balanced.'],
['A model threshold is tightened. What should a business assess jointly?','Missed events and review costs','Only positive-alert precision','Only the number of charts','Only training elapsed time','Thresholds trade off error types and operational capacity; one rate is insufficient.'],
['Two regions share the same conditional test rates but differ in prevalence. What can differ?','Positive predictive value','The algebra of Bayes’ rule','The definition of an event','The test’s output vocabulary','Bayes’ posterior changes with the prior mixture even when likelihood rates match.']
]},
{id:'ps-dependence',comps:['m1.c1','m1.c2'],title:'Separate dependence, aggregation and causal claims',intro:'A support team sees different success rates across channels. Before acting, ask who enters each channel, whether repeat observations are independent and whether the aggregate hides subgroup differences.',sections:[['Condition on relevant groups without inventing causes','Overall rates are weighted mixtures of subgroup rates. A channel serving more difficult cases can have a lower aggregate success rate even when within comparable groups it performs well. Simpson-type reversals arise when group weights differ; they do not tell us automatically which comparison is causally appropriate.','Use domain knowledge to choose comparisons. Adjusting for a variable affected by the intervention can distort the desired effect, while omitting a genuine confounder can also mislead. A causal question needs a study design and assumptions, not only additional slicing.'],['Sampling units determine uncertainty','Ten visits from one customer do not necessarily provide ten independent customer outcomes. Repeated measures, shared households, common outages and regional clustering can introduce dependence. Standard errors based on independent rows may then be too small.','Define the unit of assignment, analysis and decision. Report subgroup rates with counts and a documented weighting policy. The fixture demonstrates a weighting contrast using exact counts; it does not select a universal adjustment strategy or establish a treatment effect.']],problem:'Compare two groups with very different mix proportions.',steps:['Compute each channel’s group-specific rates.','Aggregate successes and denominators separately.','Compare the observed overall ordering with subgroup ordering.'],code:'groups={"A":[(81,90),(2,10)],"B":[(9,10),(24,90)]}\nfor name,rows in groups.items():\n    print(name,[round(s/n,3) for s,n in rows],round(sum(s for s,n in rows)/sum(n for s,n in rows),3))',expect:'A [0.9, 0.2] 0.83\nB [0.9, 0.267] 0.33',limit:'A wins overall despite not outperforming B in either displayed group because group weights differ. This is descriptive aggregation, not causal identification.',mistakes:['Averaging group percentages equally ignores denominator weights.','Repeated rows may not be independent evidence.'],takeaways:['Inspect mixtures and analysis units.','Association and causal effects need different evidence.'],practice:[
['How is an aggregate success rate computed from groups?','Total successes/total cases','Mean of every percentage always','Largest group percentage','Total cases/total successes','Combine counts before division; an unweighted mean only matches under special group sizes.'],
['Can subgroup composition change an overall rate?','Yes','No, rates ignore weights','Only for negative counts','Only when names differ','An overall rate weights subgroup rates by their population proportions.'],
['Are repeated visits from one person necessarily independent?','No','Yes, every row is independent','Only if timestamps differ','Only if values are numeric','Shared person-level factors can correlate visits, requiring an appropriate sampling/analysis model.'],
['A support channel receives harder cases. What limits a raw success comparison?','Case-mix differences','The availability of arithmetic','The existence of identifiers','The use of percentages','Different difficulty composition can confound the interpretation of aggregate success rates.'],
['A survey doubles rows by duplicating each respondent. What has not doubled?','Independent information','Stored record count','File length','Number of row positions','Copying observations adds no new independent evidence about the population.'],
['A report finds a subgroup reversal. What should happen next?','Review design and weighting','Declare one channel causal','Discard every subgroup','Select the preferred headline','Reversals motivate investigation of populations and causal assumptions, not an automatic preferred conclusion.'],
['A model adjusts for a variable caused by treatment. What needs review?','The causal estimand/design','Only whether it is numeric','Only its column position','Only missing-value count','Conditioning on post-treatment variables can alter or bias the effect being estimated.']
],formal:[
['What makes an aggregate rate a weighted mean?','Group denominator shares','Alphabetic group order','Largest group label','Equal weights by default','Each subgroup rate contributes according to its share of the total denominator.'],
['Does correlation prove causation?','No','Yes if positive','Yes if rounded','Only if plotted','Association can arise from confounding, selection or reverse direction; causal identification needs more.'],
['What is a sampling unit?','The selected entity','Every cell in a table','Only a column heading','Always one database row','The selected entity may be a person, household, clinic or cluster rather than an arbitrary stored row.'],
['A hospital compares wards with different patient severity. Which issue matters?','Case-mix confounding','Only chart colors','Only patient-id length','Only report page count','Severity affects outcomes and group composition, so raw rates may not reflect care effects.'],
['A customer contributes fifty sessions to an experiment. What should standard-error assumptions consider?','Within-user dependence','Only total page views','Only file compression','Only unique timestamps','Related sessions may be correlated; treating them as independent can understate uncertainty.'],
['A manager averages a 1/2 rate with a 90/100 rate equally. What is the correct combined rate?','91/102','70/100','91/100','92/102','Combine 91 successes across 102 cases; equal percentage averaging ignores the unequal denominator sizes.'],
['A company changes subgroup weights and sees an overall metric rise. What is not established?','Improvement within each subgroup','The new aggregate arithmetic','That weights changed','The existence of subgroup counts','An aggregate can improve solely from composition changes without within-group improvements.'],
['An analyst chooses adjustment variables only because they improve the preferred result. What is needed?','A justified prespecified design','More decimal places','A larger slide deck','Only a significance threshold','Variable choices should follow the causal/statistical question, not outcome-driven selection.'],
['A dataset contains duplicate outcomes after a join. What may happen to estimated precision?','It can be overstated','It must be unchanged','It becomes exactly zero','It proves independent evidence','Duplicated correlated records can inflate apparent sample size and distort both rates and uncertainty.'],
['Two aggregate comparisons disagree after conditioning. What is the responsible interpretation?','Review the populations','Always prefer the larger rate','Always prefer the smaller sample','Declare arithmetic impossible','Both summaries can be arithmetically correct while answering different population-conditioned questions.']
]},
{id:'ps-distributions',comps:['m2.c1'],title:'Choose a distribution by its data-generating assumptions',intro:'A count of failed deliveries and a continuous measurement of package mass are different random variables. A distribution specifies probabilities over the values a variable can take, under explicit assumptions.',sections:[['Discrete counts and continuous measurements','A Bernoulli variable records one binary outcome with success probability p. A binomial count sums n independent Bernoulli trials with the same p; its mean is np and variance np(1−p). Independence and a common probability are assumptions, not properties guaranteed by a binary column.','A Poisson model describes nonnegative event counts with mean λ and equal variance λ under its model. It can be useful for arrivals over a fixed exposure, but clustering, changing rates and overdispersion challenge it. Always define exposure units, such as requests per minute or failures per device-hour.'],['Normal shape is a model, not a universal default','A normal distribution is continuous, symmetric and described by mean μ and standard deviation σ>0. Values can range over the whole real line. Counts, bounded proportions, positive skewed durations and multimodal populations may need other models or transformations.','A density value at a point is not the probability of that exact continuous value; probabilities come from intervals. The fixture calculates a binomial probability directly and a normal interval using Python’s documented NormalDist API. Those calculations do not validate the model assumptions for real deliveries.']],problem:'Calculate a binomial event probability and a normal interval probability.',steps:['State independent equal-probability trials for the binomial model.','Compute exactly two successes among three trials.','Evaluate a symmetric standard-normal interval.'],code:'import math\nfrom statistics import NormalDist\np=0.2\nprint(round(math.comb(3,2)*p**2*(1-p),3))\nN=NormalDist()\nprint(round(N.cdf(1)-N.cdf(-1),4))',expect:'0.096\n0.6827',limit:'These are calculations under declared models, not evidence that a real event process is independent or normally distributed.',mistakes:['Binary outcomes alone do not establish binomial independence.','A continuous density at a point is not a point-event probability.'],takeaways:['Match support, units and assumptions.','Distinguish mean, variance, density and interval probability.'],practice:[
['Which model describes one binary trial?','Bernoulli','Normal','Uniform continuous only','Any matrix','A Bernoulli variable has outcomes zero and one with a specified success probability.'],
['What is the mean of a binomial(n,p) count?','np','n/p','p/n','n+p','A sum of n equal-probability Bernoulli indicators has expected count np.'],
['What does a normal CDF return?','Cumulative probability','Density at one exact point','The sample maximum','A causal effect','The cumulative distribution function integrates probability up to the specified value.'],
['Ticket arrivals cluster after outages. Which Poisson assumption deserves review?','Independent stable-rate arrivals','A positive exposure duration','The use of count outcomes','A named support queue','Clustering and rate changes can violate the simple Poisson process assumptions and inflate variability.'],
['A delivery duration cannot be negative and is strongly skewed. What should be questioned?','A naive normal model','The existence of a mean','The meaning of an hour','The use of numeric storage','A normal distribution assigns mass to negative values and is symmetric, which may poorly fit this duration process.'],
['Three independent trials have p=0.2. What is P(exactly two successes)?','0.096','0.008','0.512','0.6','There are three placements for two successes, yielding 3×0.2²×0.8=0.096.'],
['A normal density exceeds one for a very narrow distribution. Is that alone invalid?','No, interval probability matters','Yes, every density is a probability','Yes, means must exceed one','Only if values are positive','Densities are not point probabilities; their integral over the full support must equal one.']
],formal:[
['Which distribution is discrete?','Binomial','Normal','Continuous uniform','Continuous exponential','A binomial variable takes integer counts from zero through n.'],
['For binomial(n,p), what is the variance?','np(1−p)','np²','n+p','p(1−n)','Independent equal-probability Bernoulli variances sum to np(1−p).'],
['What does a probability density determine through integration?','Interval probability','Sample identifier','A fixed row count','Universal causality','For continuous variables, integrating density over an interval yields its probability.'],
['A QA team models ten correlated retries as independent Bernoulli trials. What is at risk?','The binomial uncertainty model','The ability to count failures','The existence of binary values','The number ten itself','Dependence changes count variability and invalidates an unexamined binomial assumption.'],
['A call-center count has variance far above its mean. What should be reviewed?','Simple Poisson adequacy','Only the metric label','Only the report timestamp','Only integer storage','A Poisson model constrains variance to equal its mean; overdispersion suggests missing structure or another model.'],
['A warehouse compares arrival counts over one hour and eight hours. What must be aligned?','Exposure duration','Only bar colors','Only count column names','Only data export format','Counts depend on the observation exposure; rate comparisons need comparable units or an exposure adjustment.'],
['A model assigns negative waiting times under a normal approximation. What is the concern?','Support mismatch','Too many discrete categories','A missing binary label','The impossibility of a mean','Waiting times are nonnegative; a normal model may assign unrealistic probability to invalid outcomes.'],
['A customer count is modeled as binomial with p varying strongly by customer. What assumption fails?','Common trial probability','Finite count support','The existence of a sample','The fact of a binary outcome','The simple binomial model assumes equal success probability across its independent trials.'],
['A report treats the density at exactly 20kg as a probability. What should replace it?','A measurement interval','A larger density label','The same number as percent','The full population count','For a continuous model an exact point has probability zero; a measurement interval has meaningful mass.'],
['An analyst selects a distribution solely because its name is familiar. What evidence is needed?','Support/process checks','Only a fitted mean','Only chart aesthetics','Only a nonempty sample','A useful model must match the variable’s support and relevant generating assumptions, then be checked against observations.']
]},
{id:'ps-sampling',comps:['m2.c2'],title:'Design samples that support a target population claim',intro:'A large dataset can still misrepresent a customer population. Sampling design controls who has a chance to appear and what inference the observations can support.',sections:[['Representativeness comes from selection, not size alone','A simple random sample selects units under a known random design. Convenience samples, voluntary feedback and logs missing offline behavior can overrepresent particular groups. Increasing a biased sample’s size reduces some random variation but does not remove systematic selection bias.','Stratified sampling divides a population into specified groups and samples within them. Population estimates may need weights reflecting sampling probabilities or population shares. Cluster sampling selects groups such as stores; observations within a selected group may be dependent, changing the uncertainty calculation.'],['Separate target, frame and observed responses','The target population is the group the decision concerns. The sampling frame is the available list or mechanism used to select units. Noncoverage occurs when target units are absent from the frame; nonresponse occurs when selected units do not supply usable observations.','Record eligibility, time window and response handling. The worked example shows a weighted mean across two known strata and contrasts an unweighted response mix. It assumes stratum means are appropriate estimates and the stated population weights are correct; those assumptions still require evidence.']],problem:'Estimate a population mean from two unequal strata rather than response proportions.',steps:['Record population shares independently of response counts.','Combine stratum means with population weights.','Contrast the naive response-weighted estimate.'],code:'means=[10,30]\npopulation=[0.8,0.2]\nresponses=[20,80]\nprint(sum(m*w for m,w in zip(means,population)))\nprint(sum(m*n for m,n in zip(means,responses))/sum(responses))',expect:'14.0\n26.0',limit:'Correct supplied weights fix this illustrated composition difference, not unknown within-stratum nonresponse or measurement bias.',mistakes:['Many records from a selective frame do not guarantee representativeness.','Weighting cannot automatically fix missing groups or all measurement error.'],takeaways:['Define the target population and selection mechanism.','Use uncertainty methods consistent with the sampling design.'],practice:[
['Does a large convenience sample guarantee representativeness?','No','Yes once n exceeds one hundred','Yes if values are numeric','Only if every row has an id','Systematic selection bias can persist regardless of sample size.'],
['What is a sampling frame?','The selection frame','The report border style','Every possible business question','Only the final response count','The frame operationalizes which units can enter the sample.'],
['What does stratification do?','Samples within defined groups','Deletes every small group','Forces equal outcomes','Makes all observations causal','Stratification organizes selection within known groups and may require design-based weights.'],
['A mobile-only survey claims all-customer preferences. What is a likely issue?','Frame noncoverage','Guaranteed random selection','A missing arithmetic operator','A universally normal outcome','Customers unable or unwilling to use mobile may be absent from the sampling frame.'],
['Two strata have means 10 and 30 and population shares 80% and 20%. What is the weighted mean?','14','20','26','40','The population-weighted mean is 0.8×10+0.2×30=14.'],
['A team samples entire stores. What should its analysis consider?','Within-store correlation','Only total receipt count','Only storage format','Only cashier names','Clustered units can share conditions, so treating every receipt as independent can misstate precision.'],
['Response weights are known but one subgroup never responds. What is still unresolved?','Its unobserved outcome behavior','The definition of addition','The existence of respondents','The ability to store weights','Weighting needs assumptions or information about missing groups; it cannot reveal unobserved outcomes by itself.']
],formal:[
['What is the target population?','The inference target','Only observed respondents','Only the database keys','The largest available file','The target specifies whose behavior or outcomes the decision aims to describe.'],
['Which issue is nonresponse?','Selected units provide no usable data','Target units are absent from the frame','A metric has a denominator','Two groups have different means','Nonresponse occurs after selection when the selected unit’s required information is unavailable.'],
['Can a small random sample have sampling error?','Yes','No, randomness means exactness','Only when outcomes are negative','Only when groups are named','Random designs support uncertainty quantification; they do not make every sample equal the population.'],
['A retailer surveys only loyalty-card users. What limits a claim about all shoppers?','Selective coverage','The presence of price data','The use of percentages','The number of product names','Nonmembers may differ and have no chance to appear under that frame.'],
['A clinic deliberately oversamples a rare age group. What may a population estimate need?','Appropriate sampling weights','Equal weights automatically','Deletion of the rare group','Random replacement of ages','Unequal selection probabilities require a corresponding weighting/design treatment for target-population estimates.'],
['A platform receives millions of voluntary ratings. Which claim is unsupported?','They necessarily represent silent users','The database has many rows','Respondents supplied ratings','The observed average can be computed','Voluntary participation may select systematically different users from nonrespondents.'],
['A transport survey samples depots then drivers within depots. What should uncertainty reflect?','The multistage clustered design','Only the last row count','Only report dimensions','Only positive response values','Sampling stages and shared depot conditions affect effective independent information.'],
['An analyst changes target from current users to all residents. What must be revisited?','Frame coverage','Only chart title wording','Only data export filename','Only metric decimal places','A different target may contain units not represented by the original selection mechanism.'],
['A survey has known population weights but inaccurate self-reported income. What remains?','Measurement error','A solved accuracy problem','Guaranteed unbiased outcomes','A need to sort names','Weights address selection composition under assumptions, not mismeasurement of recorded values.'],
['A manager wants to fix systematic noncoverage by collecting more of the same online respondents. What is missing?','Access to excluded populations','Only a larger numeric type','Only more repeated questions','Only a faster aggregation','More data from the same restricted frame does not give excluded units a chance to appear.']
]},
{id:'ps-clt',comps:['m2.c1','m2.c2'],title:'Distinguish observations, sample means and normal approximations',intro:'Individual order values can be skewed while averages over suitable independent samples are more regular. The central limit theorem concerns a sampling distribution of averages, not a promise that every raw dataset is normal.',sections:[['Keep three distributions distinct','A population distribution describes individual outcomes. An empirical distribution summarizes the observed values. A sampling distribution describes how a statistic, such as a mean, varies across repeated samples from a specified design. Confusing these objects produces incorrect uncertainty claims.','Under independent identically distributed observations with finite variance, the standardized sample mean approaches a normal distribution as sample size grows. The mean’s standard deviation is σ/√n in this setting. Skewness, heavy tails and dependence affect how useful an approximation is at a finite n. There is no universal n=30 guarantee.'],['Standard deviation versus standard error','Standard deviation describes spread of individual observations. Standard error describes variability of an estimator across repeated samples. For an independent sample mean, s/√n is a common estimated standard error, with assumptions and small-sample limitations.','The fixture enumerates all equally likely samples of two independent Bernoulli trials to show the sampling distribution exactly. It is not a simulation claiming convergence from one run. Increasing n can reduce random error while leaving selection bias or measurement bias unchanged.']],problem:'Enumerate the distribution of a two-trial sample mean.',steps:['List equally likely independent binary outcomes.','Compute the mean for each ordered pair.','Count repeated means and compare individual versus mean variability.'],code:'from itertools import product\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom statistics import pvariance\nmeans=[sum(x)/2 for x in product([0,1],repeat=2)]\nprint(sorted(Counter(means).items()))\nprint(pvariance([0,1]), pvariance(means))',expect:'[(0.0, 1), (0.5, 2), (1.0, 1)]\n0.25 0.125',limit:'The enumerated finite distribution is exact under two independent fair binary trials. It is not normally distributed and does not verify arbitrary large-sample approximations.',mistakes:['The CLT concerns a statistic’s sampling distribution, not normal raw observations.','A standard error formula for independent rows can fail under dependence.'],takeaways:['Name the random object being distributed.','Check finite-variance, dependence and finite-sample limitations.'],practice:[
['What does the CLT primarily concern?','A standardized sample mean','Every raw observation','Every feature label','A guaranteed causal effect','Under appropriate conditions it describes the limiting sampling distribution of a standardized mean.'],
['For independent observations with standard deviation σ, what is the mean’s standard error?','σ/√n','σ×n','σ/n²','σ+n','Averaging independent contributions reduces variance to σ²/n and standard deviation to σ/√n.'],
['Does n=30 guarantee a good normal approximation for every population?','No','Yes universally','Only if a chart is used','Only for currency values','Approximation quality depends on tails, skewness, dependence and the statistic, not one universal cutoff.'],
['A team uses the standard deviation of purchases as the standard error of their mean. What differs?','Two kinds of spread','Only display notation','Only database column order','Nothing under any design','Individual outcomes vary more widely than their average under appropriate independence assumptions.'],
['A sample quadruples under the independent finite-variance model. What happens to the mean’s standard error?','It halves','It quarters','It doubles','It remains fixed','The square-root sample-size scaling gives σ/√(4n)=half the original standard error.'],
['A huge sample is selected from a biased frame. What does the CLT not remove?','Selection bias','The ability to average','All numerical precision','The existence of observations','A normal approximation for sampling variation does not correct a systematically wrong target/frame.'],
['A process has strongly dependent observations. Which calculation needs review?','The independent-mean SE','The count of stored rows','The spelling of outcome names','The existence of a sample','Dependence changes the variance of the average through covariance terms.']
],formal:[
['What does a sampling distribution describe?','Repeated-sample statistic','Only one sorted dataset','The names of selected units','Only population categories','It describes variability of an estimator under repeated sampling from a specified design.'],
['Which is typically smaller under independent averaging?','Standard error of the mean','Population support','Total sample count','The number of observed rows','A mean averages independent fluctuations, giving standard error σ/√n.'],
['Does the CLT require raw observations to be normal in its usual finite-variance form?','No','Yes by definition','Only when n is even','Only for binary data','The theorem can apply to many nonnormal populations under its assumptions.'],
['An analyst observes skewed orders and declares means can never use normal approximations. What is missing?','Sampling distributions','Only another chart color','Only sorted order values','Only a different currency','Averages can have approximately normal sampling distributions even when individual values are skewed.'],
['A service’s errors cluster during outages. What may invalidate σ/√n with row count n?','Dependence between errors','The use of numeric values','The existence of a mean','The positive sample count','Correlated observations add covariance contributions, changing the estimator variance.'],
['A manager wants half the standard error under the same independent variance. Roughly how much sample is needed?','Four times as much','Twice as much','Half as much','The same amount','Because standard error scales as 1/√n, halving it requires fourfold n.'],
['An empirical histogram from one sample is labeled a sampling distribution of its mean. What is confused?','Observation vs estimator','Only labels and colors','Only count and currency units','Nothing statistical','The sampling distribution concerns repeated-sample means, not the observations inside one sample.'],
['A heavy-tailed population has infinite variance under its model. What needs caution?','The usual finite-variance CLT','The definition of an observation','The existence of categories','The ability to record a value','The common iid finite-variance version does not supply its usual guarantee when variance is infinite.'],
['A report says a narrow standard error proves an unbiased measurement. What is wrong?','Precision is not accuracy','Standard errors cannot be small','Every mean is biased','Large samples remove all error','An estimator can vary little while systematically missing the target because of bias.'],
['A central-limit approximation passes a classroom example. What is still needed for a production analysis?','Checks of actual design and tails','Only a copied sample-size rule','Only more decimal printing','Only a normal curve image','The applicability and finite-sample accuracy depend on the real sampling process and outcome distribution.']
]},
{id:'ps-inference',comps:['m3.c1'],title:'Interpret confidence intervals and p-values precisely',intro:'A business estimate should communicate both its magnitude and uncertainty. Confidence intervals and p-values answer different statistical questions and should not be turned into automatic truth labels.',sections:[['A confidence level describes a procedure','A frequentist 95% confidence procedure produces intervals covering the fixed population parameter in 95% of repeated samples under the model/design assumptions. After observing one interval, the parameter is fixed; the confidence level is not automatically a posterior probability that this specific interval contains it.','A large-sample normal interval for a mean with known population standard deviation uses estimate ± z×σ/√n. Replacing σ with an estimated s and using a normal critical value is an approximation; small-sample settings may require a t model or other methods. Clustering, selection and measurement bias are not fixed by a formula.'],['A p-value conditions on a null model','A p-value is the probability, under the null hypothesis and stated analysis, of results at least as incompatible with the null as the observed test statistic. It is not the probability the null is true, the probability results arose by chance, or the size/importance of an effect.','Report estimates, intervals, design and practical thresholds. A small p-value can accompany a negligible effect in a huge sample; a large p-value can reflect low power rather than equivalence. The fixture computes a known-σ interval and a two-sided standard-normal tail under explicitly stated assumptions.']],problem:'Compute a normal mean interval and a two-sided null tail probability.',steps:['Calculate the standard error from known σ and n.','Apply the chosen normal critical value.','Compute both tails beyond an observed z score.'],code:'from statistics import NormalDist\nN=NormalDist()\nmean,sigma,n=100.,10.,100\nse=sigma/n**0.5\nz=N.inv_cdf(0.975)\nprint(round(mean-z*se,3),round(mean+z*se,3))\nprint(round(2*(1-N.cdf(2)),4))',expect:'98.04 101.96\n0.0455',limit:'The calculation assumes the specified known-variance/normal approximation model. It does not validate a real sample design, estimate a posterior or establish business significance.',mistakes:['A p-value is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true.','Failure to reject is not proof of equivalence or no effect.'],takeaways:['Separate uncertainty, effect size and decision value.','State model and sampling assumptions alongside intervals.'],practice:[
['What does a frequentist confidence level describe?','Long-run interval coverage','A posterior probability automatically','The fraction of raw rows retained','A guaranteed effect direction','Coverage is a property of the repeated-sampling interval procedure under its assumptions.'],
['A p-value is conditional on what?','The null model and analysis','The alternative being true','The business being profitable','The observed sample being infinite','The tail probability is calculated under the stated null hypothesis/model and test procedure.'],
['Does p>0.05 prove no effect?','No','Yes for every study','Only if n is even','Only if the mean is positive','A nonrejection can reflect limited power and does not demonstrate equivalence.'],
['A million-user experiment finds a tiny effect with a small p-value. What should be assessed?','Practical importance','Only whether p is below 0.05','Only sample filename','Only decimal precision','Statistical detectability is distinct from whether the effect is useful relative to costs and risks.'],
['An interval method ignores clustered sampling. What can be wrong?','Its coverage/width','The concept of a parameter','Every observation value','The sign of the sample count','Incorrect independence assumptions can understate uncertainty and invalidate nominal coverage.'],
['A report says p=0.03 means a 3% chance the null is true. What is incorrect?','Reversed conditioning','Only the rounding','Only the hypothesis name','The use of decimals','The p-value conditions on the null; reversing that condition requires a different inferential framework and assumptions.'],
['Two means have overlapping intervals. What should compare their difference?','A contrast analysis','The picture alone always','Only the wider interval','Only the larger mean','The uncertainty of a difference depends on the joint design/covariance and cannot always be inferred from overlap alone.']
],formal:[
['What is a confidence interval intended to quantify?','Uncertainty in an estimate','Only raw-data spread','A guaranteed causal effect','A probability of profit','An interval procedure characterizes estimation uncertainty under stated model/design assumptions.'],
['What does a small p-value indicate under the test assumptions?','Null incompatibility','The null’s posterior probability','Guaranteed practical value','Certain replication','It measures how extreme the statistic is under the specified null, not those stronger claims.'],
['Which factor usually narrows a mean interval under the same independent variance model?','Larger sample size','More chart colors','A larger file identifier','A renamed hypothesis','The estimated mean’s sampling standard error decreases approximately with the square root of sample size.'],
['A team claims a 95% interval contains 95% of individual customers. What is confused?','Parameter and outcome intervals','Only percentages and decimals','Only unit conversion','The meaning of a sample id','A confidence interval for a mean concerns the parameter, not the spread of individual outcomes.'],
['An analyst obtains p=0.2 and declares two products equivalent. What is needed?','An equivalence design','Only a larger p-value','Only a smaller font','Only equal sample names','Nonrejection of zero difference is not evidence that effects lie within a practically acceptable equivalence margin.'],
['A clinical-style analysis uses a normal critical value with tiny n and unknown variance. What requires review?','Interval assumptions','Only the mean formula','Only storage precision','Only the outcome title','Estimated variance and finite-sample behavior can require a t-based or other appropriate method.'],
['A promotion yields a precise but negative net-value estimate despite a small p-value. What governs the business decision?','Effect, cost and risk','Only significance status','Only sample size','Only the null label','A detectable effect can still be undesirable; decisions require magnitude, value, risk and uncertainty.'],
['A dashboard publishes only significant findings from many metrics. What is at risk?','Selective inference','A valid denominator always','The existence of arithmetic','Every metric’s storage type','Selection among many analyses changes error behavior and hides the full evidence/search process.'],
['A nominal interval misses due to systematic survey noncoverage. What should be acknowledged?','Sampling bias is not covered automatically','A larger z fixes all bias','Coverage guarantees all populations','The parameter becomes random by default','The usual interval accounts for its modeled random error, not every systematic mismatch with the target population.'],
['A study reports p without a test definition or stopping rule. What is missing?','The analysis specification','Only the company name','Only a chart legend','Only the output filename','A p-value has meaning relative to its hypothesis, statistic, sampling assumptions and analysis/stopping procedure.']
]},
{id:'ps-abdesign',comps:['m3.c2'],title:'Design an A/B test around a decision and assignment unit',intro:'An A/B test compares outcomes under randomized alternatives. Its usefulness starts before data collection: define the intervention, unit, population, metric and decision rule.',sections:[['Randomize the right entity and preserve assignment','Random assignment aims to make treatment independent of potential outcomes under the design. A user-level assignment should be stable across that user’s sessions. Randomizing page views while measuring user conversion can create interference, mixed exposure and correlated outcomes.','Define eligibility, exclusions, exposure, intention-to-treat versus other estimands and the observation window before inspecting results. Track assignment integrity and sample-ratio mismatch; instrumentation failures can invalidate an otherwise elegant comparison. Randomization does not repair broken measurement.'],['Plan metrics, guardrails and stopping','Choose one primary outcome that supports the decision, relevant guardrails such as error rate or refunds, and a minimally useful effect. Plan sample size/power using plausible variance, baseline and effect assumptions. A low-powered study can miss important effects.','Set the stopping/analysis rule in advance. Repeatedly checking ordinary fixed-horizon p-values and stopping at the first significant result inflates false-positive risk. Valid sequential procedures need their own prespecified design. The fixture illustrates stable assignment only, not the entire experiment platform or a valid power calculation.']],problem:'Assign repeated synthetic user visits consistently using an explicit stable rule.',steps:['Choose the user as the assignment unit.','Apply the same deterministic hash rule to each visit.','Check that repeated identities retain their group.'],code:'import hashlib\ndef arm(user):\n    b=hashlib.sha256(("trial-v1:"+user).encode()).digest()[0]\n    return "A" if b%2==0 else "B"\nvisits=["u1","u2","u1","u3","u2"]\nassigned=[(u,arm(u)) for u in visits]\nprint(all(len({g for v,g in assigned if v==u})==1 for u in set(visits)))\nprint(len(assigned),len(set(visits)))',expect:'True\n5 3',limit:'Stable hashing illustrates consistent assignment but does not establish population balance, concealment, randomization validity, instrumentation quality or treatment-effect identification by itself.',mistakes:['Randomizing sessions while analyzing users can mix exposure and violate assumptions.','Peeking with fixed-horizon thresholds changes error rates.'],takeaways:['Choose assignment and analysis units deliberately.','Prespecify metrics, guardrails and stopping.'],practice:[
['What is an assignment unit?','The entity randomized','Every chart point','The hypothesis title','Only the outcome column','The unit is the entity allocated to a treatment condition, such as a user or store.'],
['Why keep user assignment stable across visits?','Avoid mixed exposure','Force identical outcomes','Remove all sampling error','Guarantee causal fairness','Stable assignment keeps a user in one intended intervention condition across repeated visits.'],
['What is a guardrail metric?','A monitored harm/constraint outcome','A second name for every p-value','Only the largest positive metric','The experiment identifier','Guardrails monitor important adverse effects or constraints while the primary metric evaluates benefit.'],
['A team stops an ordinary fixed-horizon test at its first p<0.05. What is at risk?','Inflated false-positive error','Guaranteed lower bias','Exact original error control','Automatic equivalence','Repeated unadjusted looks with outcome-dependent stopping invalidate the nominal fixed-horizon testing error.'],
['A treatment changes checkout but conversion events stop logging in one arm. What failed?','Measurement integrity','The arithmetic of subtraction','The possibility of an outcome','Only the report format','Differential instrumentation can create apparent effects unrelated to the actual business outcome.'],
['A store-level intervention affects all shoppers in a store. Which unit may need consideration?','Store/cluster assignment','Each line item independently','Each decimal digit','Every dashboard refresh','Shared exposure and interference can make cluster-level randomization/analysis appropriate.'],
['An experiment has little power for its minimum useful effect. What is a likely limitation?','Important effects can be missed','Every significant result is false','Randomization is impossible','The primary metric is undefined','Low power reduces the chance of detecting effects at the planned size under the model.']
],formal:[
['What does random assignment aim to support?','Comparable intervention groups','Identical outcomes by force','Universal sample representativeness','Perfect instrumentation','Randomization supports causal comparison under its design assumptions; it does not guarantee every other property.'],
['When should the primary metric be specified?','Before outcome inspection','After finding significance','Only after release','After discarding losing metrics','Prespecification reduces selective reporting and connects the analysis to the original decision.'],
['What is statistical power?','Detection probability','Probability the null is true','Observed conversion rate','Fraction assigned to control','Power is the rejection probability under a stated alternative, design and test procedure.'],
['A user sees A on one device and B on another. What should the experiment review?','Identity/exposure policy','Only page color','Only total event count','Only the test filename','Cross-device identity or assignment handling can mix intervention exposure and distort the intended estimand.'],
['A pricing experiment affects inventory available to both arms. What is a concern?','Interference between units','A necessarily normal metric','An invalid count type','Guaranteed independent effects','One unit’s treatment can change outcomes available to another, violating simple no-interference assumptions.'],
['A test has a major unexpected sample-ratio mismatch. What should happen before declaring impact?','Check assignment/data','Ignore it if p is small','Rename the arms','Delete the control group','Unexpected allocation imbalance can signal assignment, eligibility or logging failures.'],
['A team chooses its success metric after seeing twenty outcomes. What should be disclosed?','The outcome-driven search','Only the winning p-value','Only the largest lift','Only the treatment label','Post-hoc metric selection changes inference and should not be represented as a prespecified confirmatory test.'],
['A company wants to monitor continuously with valid error control. What is appropriate?','A designed sequential procedure','Repeated ordinary tests unchanged','Stopping at the best result','Only doubling decimal precision','Sequential inference requires a compatible procedure and stopping plan, not unrestricted fixed-horizon peeking.'],
['A checkout test improves clicks but raises completed-order errors. What matters for release?','Primary and guardrail outcomes','Only the click uplift','Only the positive p-value','Only the treatment screenshot','A decision must consider actual business benefit and harms, not one attractive intermediate metric.'],
['A hashing fixture keeps assignments stable. What remains unproved?','Experiment validity','That repeated hashes match','That ids are inputs','That groups have labels','Stability is one implementation property; valid causal testing also needs design, measurement and analysis evidence.']
]},
{id:'ps-abread',comps:['m3.c1','m3.c2'],title:'Read experiment effects, uncertainty and release tradeoffs',intro:'An A/B result should describe what changed, for whom, by how much and with what uncertainty. A percent uplift without its baseline can mislead a product decision.',sections:[['Absolute differences and relative uplift answer different questions','If conversion rises from 10% to 12%, the absolute change is two percentage points and the relative uplift is 20%. Neither number alone specifies uncertainty or value. Record denominators, eligible population, period and whether the unit is a user, session or transaction.','For independent arms, a simple large-sample difference-of-proportions standard error uses the sum of estimated arm variances. That approximation needs sufficient data and a design compatible with independent units. Repeated users, clusters, rare events and sequential stopping may require other methods.'],['Decide with evidence rather than a significance badge','Interpret a confidence interval relative to zero and the minimum practically useful effect. An interval can include both harm and worthwhile benefit, leaving the decision uncertain. A statistically detectable difference can still be too small to justify cost; an inconclusive result does not establish equivalence.','Subgroup and secondary-metric exploration can generate hypotheses but requires appropriate multiplicity treatment and validation before strong claims. The worked numbers describe one synthetic fixed-horizon comparison under a large-sample approximation. A production decision still needs data-quality checks, cost/risk assessment and the prespecified analysis.']],problem:'Compute absolute/relative change and a rough independent-arm interval.',steps:['Compute each arm’s observed conversion rate.','Separate percentage-point change from relative lift.','Calculate the stated large-sample standard error and interval.'],code:'import math\npa,pb=100/1000,120/1000\ndiff=pb-pa\nse=math.sqrt(pa*(1-pa)/1000+pb*(1-pb)/1000)\nprint(round(100*diff,2),round(100*diff/pa,2))\nprint(round(diff-1.96*se,4),round(diff+1.96*se,4))',expect:'2.0 20.0\n-0.0074 0.0474',limit:'The rough interval includes zero and assumes independent arms and an appropriate fixed-horizon normal approximation. It does not prove equivalence or authorize release.',mistakes:['Two percentage points is not the same as a two-percent relative lift.','Selecting favorable subgroups after results can overstate evidence.'],takeaways:['Report baseline, absolute effect, relative effect and uncertainty.','Align release decisions with meaningful benefit and guardrails.'],practice:[
['Conversion rises from 10% to 12%. What is the absolute change?','2 percentage points','2 percent relative','20 percentage points','120 percent relative','Subtract rates to obtain 0.02, or two percentage points; relative uplift is twenty percent.'],
['What is relative uplift from 10% to 12%?','20%','2%','12%','200%','Divide the absolute increase 0.02 by the original rate 0.10.'],
['An interval includes meaningful harm and meaningful benefit. What does it convey?','Decision uncertainty','Proved equivalence','Guaranteed positive effect','Guaranteed no effect','The data/design do not narrowly distinguish these practically different effects.'],
['A large experiment detects a tiny profitable lift. What should the decision incorporate?','Value, cost and guardrails','Only whether p is small','Only relative lift','Only treatment page speed','Statistical evidence and practical value must be assessed together with constraints and harms.'],
['The same user contributes outcomes to both arms. What should be reviewed?','Dependence/design assumptions','Only the conversion labels','Only the chart legend','Only numeric output type','An independent-arm variance formula may not match overlapping or paired observations.'],
['Twenty exploratory subgroups are tested and only one winner is shown. What is missing?','Multiplicity disclosure','Only a larger title','Only an average p-value','Only alphabetical ordering','Outcome-driven selection among multiple analyses changes error interpretation and requires transparency/appropriate methods.'],
['The test is nonsignificant. Can the team claim equal products?','Not from nonrejection alone','Yes for all metrics','Yes if arms are named A/B','Only with positive conversions','Equivalence requires a prespecified acceptable margin and a suitable design/analysis.']
],formal:[
['A rate rises from 4% to 5%. What is the relative increase?','25%','1%','5%','125%','The one-percentage-point increase divided by the original four percent equals twenty-five percent.'],
['What is a percentage-point difference?','Subtraction of two percentages','Their ratio alone','Their product','The sample size divided by two','Percentage points express the absolute difference between rates on a percent scale.'],
['Does an interval containing zero prove zero effect?','No','Yes automatically','Only if symmetric','Only if n is large','The interval represents a range compatible with the procedure; inclusion of zero is not proof of exact equality.'],
['A revenue metric improves but refund harm breaches a guardrail. What should release consider?','Benefit/harm policy','Only the revenue headline','Only the smallest p-value','Only the largest sample group','Decision policy should account for important adverse outcomes, not just the favorable primary estimate.'],
['A 50% relative lift comes from 0.2% to 0.3%. What should be shown too?','The 0.1-point absolute change','Only the relative headline','Only the treatment name','Only the bar colors','The baseline and absolute change reveal scale and help estimate actual business impact.'],
['An analyst applies independent-proportion SE to store-cluster randomization. What needs checking?','Cluster-aware uncertainty','Only whether counts are positive','Only the mean formula','Only the database engine','Shared store-level outcomes can violate independence assumptions used by a row-level variance calculation.'],
['An experiment’s interval lies below the minimum useful gain despite positive estimates. What can follow?','Evidence of insufficient practical gain','Guaranteed market success','Proof of no sampling error','Automatic equivalence on all metrics','The effect may be statistically positive yet too small to meet the decision’s practical value threshold.'],
['A team selects the best of thirty metrics and reuses a single-test threshold. What is a risk?','Elevated false discoveries','Guaranteed conservative inference','No change in error behavior','A missing arithmetic operator','Multiple outcome-driven opportunities for significance require a compatible inference/reporting approach.'],
['A user-level experiment is analyzed per session without accounting for repeats. What is at risk?','The effective sample information','The existence of page visits','The conversion event name','The treatment assignment label','Repeated sessions can be correlated and overweight frequent users, changing both estimand and uncertainty.'],
['A rough normal interval includes zero, while a manager wants an equivalence claim. What is required?','An equivalence margin','A wider chart axis','Only additional rounding','Only relabeling confidence','Equivalence is a distinct question about lying within an acceptable effect range, with its own design and inference.']
]}
];
module.exports=course('probability-statistics',specs,['statistics','math','fractions']);
